{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["Our Sages (Mekhilta here; Zevaḥim 116a) already disagreed concerning this pericope. Some of them say that Jethro came before the giving of the Torah, in the sequence of the pericopes, and some of them said that he came after the giving of the Torah. And this is certainly supported by the verse (Exodus 18:5) which says, <b>And Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, came with his sons and his wife to Moses, to the wilderness where he was encamped there, the mountain of Elohim</b> — behold, it says that he came to him when he was encamped before Mount Sinai, where they encamped for one year, and this is the meaning of <b>where he was encamped there</b>. Moreover, it says <b>and I make known the statutes of Elohim and His teachings</b> (Exodus 18:16), which are those given to him at Mount Sinai. Moreover, for here (Exodus 18:27) it says <b>And Moses sent his father-in-law, and he went his way to his own land</b>, and this was in the second year when they journeyed from Mount Sinai, as it says in the pericope of Beha'alotekha (Numbers 10:29), \"And Moses said to Hobab the son of Reuel the Midianite, Moses' father-in-law, We are journeying,\" and there it is written (Exodus 18:30) \"And he said to him, I will not go, but to my own land and to my kindred I will go,\" and that is the departure written here, \"and he went his way to his own land\" (Exodus 18:27).<br><b>Moreover</b> they brought proof from what the verse says (Deuteronomy 1:6–7), \"YHWH our Elohim spoke to us at Horeb, saying, You have dwelt long enough at this mountain; turn and journey,\" and there (Deuteronomy 1:9) it says, \"And I spoke to you at that time, saying, I am not able to bear you alone, and I took the heads of your tribes, wise men,\" etc. (ibid. 15), and this is Jethro's advice, and there (Deuteronomy 1:19) it is written, \"and we journeyed from Horeb,\" for they journeyed immediately, and if so, a reason is needed for why this pericope was written earlier here. And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 18:1) said that this was on account of the matter of Amalek, for when Scripture mentioned the evil that Amalek did to us and commanded that we requite him for his evil, it mentioned the good that Jethro did for us, to instruct us that we should repay him a good recompense, and when we come to annihilate Amalek as we are commanded, we should be careful concerning the children of the Kenite who stand with them and not add them with them, and this is what Saul said to them (1 Samuel 15:6).<br><b>Yet with all this, I raise a question against this opinion.</b> When the verse says <b>And Jethro heard all that YHWH had done for Moses and for Israel His people, that YHWH had brought Israel out of Egypt</b>, why did it not say that he heard what was done for Moses and for Israel at the giving of the Torah, which is among the great wonders done for them, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:32), \"For ask now of the former days that were before you, from the day that Elohim created man upon the earth, and from one end of heaven to the other, whether there has been anything as great as this thing, or whether anything like it has been heard; did a people ever hear the voice of Elohim speaking out of the midst of the fire, as you have heard, and live?\" And when it says <b>And Moses told his father-in-law all that YHWH had done to Pharaoh and to Egypt on account of Israel, all the hardship that had found them on the way</b> (Exodus 18:8), and Jethro said from this, \"Now I know that YHWH is great\" (Exodus 18:11), why did he not tell him about the standing at Mount Sinai, from which it would be known that HaShem is true and His Torah is true and there is none besides Him, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:35–36), \"You have been shown to know that YHWH is Elohim, there is none besides Him; from the heavens He made you hear His voice,\" etc.? And perhaps we may say that Jethro heard in his land immediately that YHWH had brought Israel out of Egypt, and he journeyed from his land and reached Moses after he was encamped at Mount Sinai, after the giving of the Torah. And Scripture did not mention that he told him about the matter of that standing, because the matter was still recent and they were still there, and it is known that it was told to him.<br><b>But that which seems right to me</b> in maintaining the sequence of the Torah is that he came before the giving of the Torah while they were in Rephidim, as they said in the Mekhilta (here): Rabbi Yehoshua says, the war with Amalek he heard and came — which is written alongside it — and he journeyed with them from Rephidim to Mount Sinai. And the verse (Exodus 18:5) that says \"to the wilderness where he was encamped there, the mountain of Elohim,\" its meaning is that Mount Sinai was on the route from Midian, close from there, since Moses went there to graze the flock from Midian (Exodus 3:1), and concerning Aaron it says, \"and he met him at the mountain of Elohim\" (Exodus 4:27). And behold, Jethro went forth with his daughter and the children from Midian and they came to Mount Sinai, and Moses was at Rephidim, which is one place in the wilderness of Sin, for the verse says (Exodus 16:1), \"And they journeyed from Elim and all the congregation of the children of Israel came to the wilderness of Sin, which is between Elim and Sinai,\" to say that the wilderness of Sin extends to Mount Sinai, and includes Dophkah and Alush and Rephidim. And even though it says (Exodus 17:1), \"And they journeyed from the wilderness of Sin and encamped in Rephidim,\" so too it says, \"And they journeyed from the wilderness of Sin and encamped in Dophkah and Alush\" (Numbers 33:12–13), and they are part of the wilderness of Sin itself, for the entire wilderness is called the wilderness of Sin, and the place before Mount Sinai is called the wilderness of Sin, and similarly \"and they returned to the land of the Philistines\" (Genesis 21:32). And behold, the meaning of the verse will be <b>And Jethro, Moses' father-in-law, came with his sons and his wife to Moses, to the wilderness where he was encamped there, and he came to the mountain of Elohim</b> — for to the mountain he came and there he stood, as when it says \"and he came to the mountain of Elohim, to Horeb,\" and similarly \"and he met him at the mountain of Elohim\" (Exodus 4:27), and similarly \"You have dwelt long enough at this mountain\" (Deuteronomy 1:6), and from the mountain he sent to him <b>I, your father-in-law Jethro, am coming to you</b>, and Moses went out to him. And there is no need to say that \"and he said\" is written out of sequence. And even if Rephidim were not in the wilderness of Sin, in any case it was in the wilderness, for Israel did not enter an inhabited land all forty years, and it was close to Mount Sinai, for they came from there to the mountain in a single day's encampment of a great host like that, as I have explained (Exodus 17:6) concerning the rock from which they drank at Rephidim as I set forth there. And behold, the explanation I have given will be correct.<br><b>And I also saw in the Mekhilta</b> (here): <b>to the wilderness where he was encamped there</b> — the verse expresses astonishment about him, that he was dwelling amidst the glory of the world and sought to go out to a desolate wilderness in which there is nothing. Their opinion is that <b>to the wilderness where he was encamped there</b> means the wilderness of <b>the mountain of Elohim</b>, for from Elim to Mount Sinai was the wilderness of Sin, and the verse relates that he came to the edge of the wilderness where he was encamped there, which is the wilderness of the mountain of Elohim, to Horeb, and it mentioned this to praise Jethro, that he left his land and came to the wilderness where he was, because he knew that it was the mountain of Elohim, for in it Elohim had been revealed to him — for he had already heard the entire matter, that Israel went out from Egypt to serve Elohim on this mountain (Exodus 3:12) — and he came for the sake of the name of YHWH, the Elohim of Israel. And this too is correct. And so it seems to me that what is said here (Exodus 18:27), \"And Moses sent his father-in-law and he went his way to his own land,\" was in the first year, and he went to his land and returned to him. And it is possible that he went there to convert his family and returned to Moses while he was still at Mount Sinai, for it is close to Midian, as I have mentioned, for behold, when the camp journeyed in Iyyar of the second year, Moses said to him, \"We are journeying; come with us\" (Numbers 10:29), and he answered him, \"I will not go, but to my own land and to my kindred I will go\" (ibid. 30) — Moses entreated him greatly and said to him, \"Please do not leave us,\" etc., \"and you shall be to us as eyes, and it shall be that when you go with us, that good which YHWH shall do kindly with us, we shall bestow kindly upon you\" (ibid. 31–32), and he did not answer him anything, and it appears that he accepted his words and did as he wished and did not abandon them. But in the days of Saul his children were with Amalek, and they came and joined Israel again, and the children of Jonadab the son of Rechab were in Jerusalem. Perhaps at the death of Moses he returned to his land, either he or his children. And it is possible that the Kenite who dwelt with Amalek was from the family of Jethro, not his children, and he did kindness with the whole family for his sake, as Joshua did with the family of Rahab (Joshua 6:23).<br><b>And the opinion of our Sages is</b> that he went with them, as they said in the Sifrei (Beha'alotekha, para. 1), that they gave him the fertile land of Jericho, and they ate from it until the Temple was built, 480 years. And Rashi himself wrote this in the pericope of Beha'alotekha (Numbers 10:32). If so, he returned to him. And in the Mekhilta (here) he said to him: I am going to convert the people of my land, that I may bring them under the wings of the heavens. One might think that he went and did not return — Scripture says, \"And the children of the Kenite, Moses' father-in-law, went up from the city of palm trees,\" etc." "<b>For Moses and for Israel His people</b> — the wonders that He did for Moses, that He did with him kindness and goodness, that he would come to Pharaoh continually and not be afraid of him, and bring the plagues upon him until they went out from Egypt, he and the people with him, and he was like a king over them. And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 18:1) said: <b>for Moses and for Israel</b> — on account of Moses and Israel, and the meaning concerns the plagues and the drowning of Pharaoh. And so it appears from the opinion of our Sages, who said (Mekhilta here) that Moses was weighted equally with Israel and Israel with Moses." "And the meaning of <b>all that Elohim had done — that YHWH had brought</b>: it mentioned Elohim, the Name that Jethro knew from before, and \"that YHWH had brought out\" — by His great Name, which became known now through Moses, by which the signs were performed."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban engages the chronological question of when Jethro arrived, surveying positions in the Mekhilta and Talmud, thenadopting the view that Jethro came before the giving of the Torah. The verse citations in parentheses reflect Ramban's own cross-references, normalized to standard format; one internal reference (שמות י\"ח:ל') pointing to Jethro's refusal appears to cite Numbers 10:30, likely a manuscript confusion preserved in the Hebrew.", 1 "The phrase 'ור\"א' abbreviates רבי אברהם, i.e., Ibn Ezra, resolved by the parenthetical citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:51.508414913Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}